DCFS DESIGN
===========

Living document. Records what we have discussed and decided, and what is
still open. Update when decisions are made or revisited.


Vision
------

A traditional turn-based roguelike, heavily influenced by DCSS.

Graphics: semi-topdown 3d perspective, 90s aesthetic — low poly, flat
shading, simple low-res textures (Alone in the Dark, 1992).

Online: every game is single player, but played against a server.
Community features are a core goal: spectating other players' games,
tournaments, leaderboards. Cheating must be impossible by construction:
the server owns all game state and the client only ever sees what the
server has told it. A client can at most know what a human spectator
could know.

Networking is NOT implemented first. But the code is structured around
it from day one: running locally means running a local server inside the
game process that talks to the client by passing messages directly
instead of through a socket.


DECIDED: client / server / protocol split
-----------------------------------------

    src/game/
        protocol/    message definitions, shared ids/enums, ser/deser
        server/      authoritative simulation (headless)
        client/      presentation: rendering, input, UI, animation
        game_main.c  local bootstrap: wires client + local server

Layering, enforced by meson include_directories (same mechanism that
hides vulkan from game_lib today):

    protocol -> core
    server   -> core, protocol          (NO engine, NO renderer headers)
    client   -> core, protocol, engine

The server library not being able to even include a renderer header is
the moat. It also means a standalone headless server binary later is
just: core + protocol + server + a network main.

The server is the game. Dungeon generation, the turn scheduler, AI,
FOV, combat resolution, item logic, RNG — all server, and the server
is always the authority. The client folds the server's message stream
into its own view of the world, draws it, and predicts ahead of the
server where it can (see prediction section) — but it only ever works
from information the server has already sent it.

"Common" code is deliberately small: protocol messages, shared ids
(terrain/monster/item types), static display-data tables (id ->
name/model/color — public knowledge, nothing hidden in them), and
pure rule predicates needed for prediction (is this terrain walkable,
...) that operate only on visible state — shared so client prediction
and server resolution cannot drift. Nothing beyond that: sharing
logic that touches non-visible state is exactly how hidden-state
leaks happen.


DECIDED: message flow
---------------------

Client -> server: commands (player intent). Discrete and small:
move dir, attack, use item, descend stairs, ... Turn-based, so low
rate and no timing sensitivity.

Server -> client: two kinds.

  - snapshot: complete visible-state dump. Sent on game start, and
    later on reconnect and spectator join.
  - events: ordered incremental stream. entity moved / took damage /
    died, cell became visible, stat change, ...

Events are semantic game facts, never text and never raw state blobs:
"entity E hit you for 3". The client renders the animation AND
composes the message-log line from the same event. The server never
sends anything the client can derive itself.

The client's authoritative view is a pure function of (snapshot +
events); prediction is an overlay on top of it that server events
confirm or correct, never a second source of truth. The pure-function
property gives us, for free:

  - spectating  = attach a second consumer to the same event stream
  - replays     = record the stream to disk, play it back
  - reconnect   = snapshot + resume stream

Turn model: DCSS-style energy system, server-side. The server is
reactive: it waits for a player command, then advances the world until
the player can act again, emitting events in order. No real-time tick.
The server never waits for client animation; the client buffers events
and plays them at its own pace (and can fast-forward when the queue is
deep, like DCSS does).

Hidden information: FOV is computed server-side; the client is only
sent cells and entities the player can currently see. Map memory
("I've seen this corridor before") is tracked per-player on the server
and sent as part of snapshot/events, so the client stays trivially dumb
and reconnect/spectate get correct memory for free.


DECIDED: local server, same thread
----------------------------------

Locally the server runs in-process on the main thread, pumped once per
frame: drain pending commands, run the simulation, emit events. Turn-
based simulation work is bursty and cheap, so this is fine, and it
keeps debugging deterministic and stack traces whole. Dungeon gen may
hitch a frame; acceptable for now.

Because client and server only talk through the transport, moving the
server to a thread or another machine later changes neither client nor
server code — only which transport is plugged in.


DECIDED: structs across the boundary, serialization only at the edge
--------------------------------------------------------------------

Messages are defined in protocol/ as plain C structs tagged by a
message type enum. Client and server always produce and consume these
structs directly. Serialization/deserialization is the network
layer's job alone: the future socket transport converts struct <->
bytes at the edge. The local in-process server passes structs
straight through and never runs any ser/deser.

The constraint this puts on message structs so the network layer can
be added later without touching them: flat POD, fixed layout, counted
inline arrays for variable-length content (inventory listings, ...),
and NEVER pointers — not into server state, not into client state.
A message is self-contained; receivers copy out what they keep.

Protocol carries a version number from day one. Single-digit cost,
saves grief the moment a real server exists.


DECIDED: client-side prediction (deliberate departure from DCSS)
----------------------------------------------------------------

DCSS online feels unresponsive; we will not copy that. The client
predicts whatever it can from knowledge it legitimately has, so input
feels instant even when the server is remote:

  - movement: the client knows the terrain it has seen and the
    entities it can see, so a legal-looking move starts instantly
    (step + animation) without waiting for the server.
  - inventory: contents are part of the player's knowledge and the
    server keeps the client's copy current, so opening and browsing
    it is pure client-side UI — no round trip at all. Only acting
    (use / drop / wield) sends a command.

The server stays authoritative. Commands carry a sequence number and
authoritative events reference the command that caused them, so the
client verifies each prediction against the server's resolution and
corrects (snaps) when it guessed wrong — e.g. the move was blocked by
something invisible.

Prediction changes nothing for anti-cheat: it runs only on
information the server already sent. Rules that prediction needs must
match the server exactly, which is why they live as shared pure
predicates in protocol/ (see layering section).

v1 prediction scope (decided): movement, inventory browsing, and
simple interactions (open door, pick up). Reconciliation policy on
misprediction (hard snap vs short corrective animation) gets decided
when it first happens in practice.

Prediction depth is hard-limited to ONE unacknowledged command
(decided). Deeper prediction is dishonest in a roguelike — after our
move the world acts, and the client cannot predict monsters, so a
second predicted move would run on state known to be stale — and it
would require rollback/re-simulate machinery. Depth 1 keeps
reconciliation binary: confirmed or snapped, never re-simulated.
Input arriving while a command is unacknowledged is queued
un-predicted (so held-key movement stays continuous, rendered at
authoritative pace); where that queue lives (client vs pipelined to
server) is decided when networking is real.

Spectators require nothing special from the client (decided): a
spectator is a normal client with input disabled. The client already
has to handle events not caused by any local command (most of the
stream is the world acting on its own), and prediction/reconciliation
only trigger on local input — so with no input both are naturally
dormant. Same client code, same event handling.


DECIDED: RNG and determinism
----------------------------

One game RNG, server-side, seeded per game. Enables seeded tournament
races (same dungeon for every entrant). The client may use its own
cosmetic RNG (idle wobble, particles) but it never touches game state.

Replays are event-stream based, not input-replay based — robust across
game-balance changes, since no simulation is re-run.


MILESTONE 0 (done): grid sandbox for camera/look/feel
-----------------------------------------------------

Pre-split scratch code, all in game_main.c: a checkered tile grid and
a player cube stepping tile-to-tile (held keys repeat, 8-dir), camera
following at a semi-topdown pitch. Tunables are defines at the top of
game_main.c (grid size, tile gap/colors, move step time, camera
pitch/distance/fov/follow speed). This exists to lock in the look and
feel before the client/server split; it dissolves into client/ when
milestone 2 starts.


MILESTONE 1 (done, anchoring deferred): animated models and weapon anchoring
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Declared done 2026-09-27. Built: the .frog loader, the two-buffer morph
path, the Blender export script, the segmented human with idle / walk_r /
walk_l / attack / bump, and per-instance playback speed. DEFERRED: the
runtime half of anchoring. Anchor names and per-keyframe transforms are
exported and loaded, but nothing interpolates them yet and no weapon is
drawn in the hand. The design below still stands; pick it up when
equipment first needs to be visible.

The grid sandbox's player cube becomes an animated human model holding
a weapon. Work: the .frog loader, the two-buffer morph-animation path
in the renderer + shader, CPU-side weapon anchoring, and the Blender
export script. Still pre-split scratch work (engine + game_main.c); the
client/server split is milestone 2. The design:

Animation technique: Quake-style keyframe (morph-target) vertex
animation. A model is a set of named animations, each a sequence of
keyframes holding a full copy of the mesh's deformed vertices. The
engine binds two keyframes as two vertex buffers plus a mix factor in
the push constant, and the vertex shader lerps position/normal between
them per vertex (or snaps, for the chunky 90s look). No skeleton, no
glTF — deliberately avoided as heavy machinery wrong for the aesthetic.

Weapon anchoring: held weapons attach via named anchors (the MD3 "tag"
idea, since there is no skeleton). An anchor is an origin + orientation
that lives at, e.g., the hand and is authored in Blender as an empty
parented to the mesh. Its transform is stored per keyframe just like the
vertices, and the runtime interpolates it with the SAME mix factor
(lerp the position, slerp the quat) to get the anchor's pose for the
current frame. The weapon is a separate model with its own draw call;
its model matrix is player_world_matrix * interpolated_anchor_transform.
Unlike the vertices, the anchor is one transform, not thousands — so it
is interpolated on the CPU per entity per frame and passed as an
ordinary per-draw uniform, not through the vertex-blend path.

The anchor is rigid — position + orientation only, no scale — and is
exported in the model's local space (the same space as the vertices).
So a weapon's size cannot come from the tag; weapons are authored at
final real-world scale relative to the wielder, and the exporter bakes
object transforms into the vertices (nothing survives into the file as
a transform — see the Blender authoring model below). One
consequence falls out for free: a per-entity UNIFORM scale in
player_world_matrix (a giant, a rat) flows into both the arm-offset and
the weapon mesh, so a held weapon scales with its wielder automatically.
Entity scale must stay uniform — a non-uniform scale would shear the
rigid anchor and break normals. If a single weapon ever needs rescaling
relative to wielders, that is a per-model factor in the display-data
table, not a field in the .frog.

File format (.frog): custom binary, replaces the earlier OBJ-sequence
idea entirely. OBJ cannot carry an anchor transform (it has no concept
of an empty or a standalone transform), and a per-keyframe OBJ sequence
duplicates the shared topology in every frame. One Blender Python script
walks the scene and writes this format directly — mesh, every animation,
and the anchor transforms per keyframe — with no OBJ intermediate and no
separate bake step: the exported file IS the runtime format.

Key structural decision: the format is committed to flat shading —
already the game's stated aesthetic — and that commitment cascades.
Flat-shaded neighbors never share vertices (each face needs its own
normal), so a flat-shaded mesh is exactly three unique vertices per
triangle. Two things follow:

  - NO index buffer. Indexing unshared vertices is the identity
    mapping — pure overhead. Geometry is a non-indexed triangle list,
    drawn unindexed; triangle_count is the mesh's one size field.
    (Coplanar neighbors could technically still share vertices, but
    the saving is marginal and not worth a second path.)
  - NO stored normals. A flat normal is fully determined by its
    triangle's three positions, so keyframes store positions only and
    the loader computes each keyframe's normals at load. This halves
    the file's dominant cost, the per-keyframe vertex data.

The per-frame / per-model split stays; its invariant is now: every
keyframe stores the same triangle count in the same order, because
morph interpolation blends corner i of frame A with corner i of frame
B. Everything else — the material palette, the per-triangle material
indices, anchor names — is stored once per model; only positions and
anchor transforms are per keyframe. The GPU path is unchanged by
normals leaving the file: the loader still builds position+normal
keyframe buffers for the shader to blend, the normals just come from
the loader instead of the exporter. (If the renderer later derives
flat normals in the fragment shader from screen-space derivatives,
normals leave the GPU buffers too — a renderer decision that never
touches the format.)

Consequences of that split, stated so exporter and loader agree:

  - Geometry lives ONLY inside keyframes, so the format requires at
    least one animation of at least one keyframe. A static model (a
    weapon, a prop) is one animation of one keyframe; the loader reads
    its mesh from keyframe 0. (This is the MD2/MD3 convention.) Both
    ends handle this: a static Blender model has no animation or
    keyframe data, so the export script synthesizes the single
    animation / single keyframe wrapper, and the parser recognizes the
    1-animation / 1-keyframe case and hands it back as a static model.
  - The .frog carries no sampled texture: surface color comes entirely
    from a per-model material palette. Each material is a named slot
    (skin, hair, eyes, ...) with a default base_color and specular_color,
    and every TRIANGLE stores a u8 index into that palette — material is
    a per-face property under flat shading (Blender assigns materials
    per face too), so per-vertex storage would just triplicate it. The palette
    colors in the file are DEFAULTS: a per-entity recolor (dynamic hair,
    tinted monster variants) overrides individual slots at runtime via the
    display-data table, so one model stays reusable across recolors. Slots
    are resolved by name, never by fixed index, so reordering materials in
    Blender cannot silently break a recolor. There is no submesh split —
    one draw covers the whole model; the loader expands the per-triangle
    index to per-vertex when building the constant vertex buffer
    (uploaded once, bound for every frame) and the shader looks up
    base/specular from the palette. Procedural surface detail (noise for a
    rough look) is a shader effect driven by object-space position, not a
    texture — if added it becomes a per-material flag and still needs no
    UVs.
  - Loop policy is deliberately NOT in the format. The client decides
    per animation (walk loops; attack/death play once) by animation id.

Blender authoring model (decided): a model may be any number of mesh
objects (head, torso, legs as separate objects); the exporter flattens
them into the single triangle list. Joining objects in Blender is
neither required nor wanted: it destroys authoring structure for zero
gain, and separate objects keep simple animations object-level (a head
bob is keyframes on the head object). Consequences:

  - The exporter bakes each object's evaluated world transform into
    its vertices at every sampled keyframe. No apply-transform
    discipline for the artist, and object-level animation falls out
    for free — it is just the world transform changing over time.
  - Object order in the file is fixed (sorted by object name), and
    every keyframe samples the same objects with the same topology —
    corner correspondence across keyframes is what morphing relies
    on. The exporter fails the export if the evaluated triangle count
    differs between keyframes (an animated topology-changing modifier
    would otherwise corrupt morphing silently).
  - Material palettes merge across objects by slot name: two objects
    both using "skin" share one palette slot — consistent with slots
    resolving by name everywhere else.
  - Anchors are scene-level: an empty is exported by its evaluated
    world transform (converted to engine space, same space as the
    baked vertices) no matter which object it is parented to. The
    exporter fails the export if that matrix is not rigid — a
    non-uniform scale anywhere in the empty's parent chain would
    smuggle shear into the "rigid" anchor.
  - Animations come from timeline markers: a marker names the
    animation starting at its frame and running to the frame before
    the next marker (or scene end). No markers: the authored-key span
    is one animation ("default"); no keys at all is the synthesized
    static single-keyframe case ("static", never sampled).
  - Keyframes are BAKED: for each animation the exporter freezes the
    fully evaluated (armature- and modifier-deformed) mesh at a set of
    frames that is the UNION of two things — a fixed-cadence grid
    (default 12 Hz, --rate to change) and the artist's authored key
    frame times that fall in range — with the range's first and last
    frame always included. The two roles:
      · The cadence is a density FLOOR. The runtime lerps vertex
        positions in straight lines, so between two stored frames a
        rotation becomes a chord, not an arc, and an under-sampled
        limb visibly collapses toward its pivot mid-blend. A fixed
        grid guarantees per-step rotation stays small (chord ~ arc)
        no matter how sparsely the artist keyed. It also doubles as
        the retro-chunkiness dial (lower for the 90s look), and
        captures Blender's easing for free since each sampled pose is
        the evaluated result at that instant.
      · The authored keys are the artist's OVERRIDE on that floor.
        Uniform sampling aliases fast events — it oversamples slow
        motion and undersamples fast, exactly backwards — so a punch
        peak between two grid steps gets softened. Keying that pose
        (a mesh key, or a bone key on an armature rig) pins it as an
        exact lerp anchor the bake is guaranteed to hit.
    --rate 0 drops the grid entirely: keyframes are then just the
    endpoints plus authored keys — the artist keys the lerp points by
    hand. Use only when the motion has no large unkeyed rotations.
    Keyframe time is (frame - range_start) / scene fps, so cadence
    never affects playback speed, and the runtime interpolates by each
    keyframe's stored time (the uneven spacing the union produces is
    fine). Integer frame stepping quantizes the grid
    (step = round(scene_fps / rate)), so the exporter reports the
    effective Hz. (Someday-optimum, not built: adaptive decimation —
    bake dense, then drop keyframes whose removal stays within a
    per-vertex error tolerance — gives the minimal keyset with no
    aliasing. The format already stores arbitrary per-keyframe times,
    so it needs no format change.)
  - Palette colors are read from each material's Principled BSDF
    Base Color (viewport diffuse color as fallback) and the viewport
    display specular color; faces with no material get a synthesized
    "default" slot. When the Base Color socket is DRIVEN by a link,
    its default_value is dead — rendering ignores it — so the exporter
    follows the link instead: an RGB node exports its value, and any
    other driver (texture, attribute, ...) fails the export, because
    the palette can only hold a flat color.

Conventions: little-endian; fields packed, no padding; a string is a
u16 byte length followed by that many UTF-8 bytes, no null terminator;
the magic is the 8 ASCII bytes "FROGMODL". Versioned from day one, same
principle as the protocol.

Exporter / loader contract:

  - Positions are in engine space: Blender's Z-up right-handed is
    converted to the engine's Y-up right-handed (HMM_LookAt_RH) on
    export.
  - Geometry is a non-indexed triangle list; the front face is
    CLOCKWISE, to match the renderer (VK_FRONT_FACE_CLOCKWISE +
    back-face cull; the CW choice comes from Vulkan's flipped-Y clip
    space). Blender and the graphics default are CCW, so the exporter
    reverses each triangle's winding (writes its three vertices in
    reverse order) on export — the loader stays dumb, same net result
    as the OBJ loader reversing at load today.
  - Normals are not stored. Per keyframe, the loader derives each
    triangle's flat normal from its stored corners —
    normalize(cross(v2 - v0, v1 - v0)), the sign that points outward
    given the CW winding — and assigns it to all three vertices. The
    exporter must drop zero-area triangles so this never normalizes a
    zero vector.
  - Quaternions are stored xyzw, normalized (Blender's wxyz
    reordered). The exporter flips signs where needed so an anchor's
    consecutive keyframe quats stay in the same hemisphere and the
    runtime slerp always takes the short arc.
  - The stream is packed, so fields land unaligned (triangle_count is
    at byte 10). The loader copies each field out (memcpy into aligned
    GPU buffers) and must NOT cast a pointer into the mapped file.
  - Per-keyframe anchor transforms correspond positionally to the
    header's anchor-name list; the name is not repeated per keyframe.
  - No bounding volume yet; add one via a version bump when culling
    needs it.

    FILE
      magic              u8[8]      "FROGMODL"
      version            u16
      triangle_count     u32        T  — 3·T unshared vertices; shared by every keyframe
      material_count     u16        P
      anchor_count       u16        A
      animation_count    u16        M

      materials  × material_count (P):
          name_len       u16
          name           u8[name_len]        slot name: "skin", "hair", ...
          base_color     f32[3]     12
          specular_color f32[3]     12

      triangle materials  × triangle_count (T):           [1 byte each]
          material       u8         1        index into the palette above

      anchor names  × anchor_count (A):
          name_len       u16
          name           u8[name_len]

      animations  × animation_count (M):
          name_len       u16
          name           u8[name_len]
          keyframe_count u16        K

          keyframes  × keyframe_count (K):
              time       f32        seconds; 0.0 for the first keyframe

              positions  × 3·T vertices, CW winding:           [12 bytes each]
                  position   f32[3]     12

              anchor transforms  × anchor_count (A):           [28 bytes each]
                  position     f32[3]   12
                  orientation  f32[4]   16   quat, xyzw


ENGINE: vertex input conventions (2026-07-25 cleanup)
-----------------------------------------------------

Vertex layouts are declared ONCE by the engine, next to the vertex
structs in mesh.h (VERTEX_LAYOUT_NORMAL, VERTEX_LAYOUT_NORMAL_MATERIAL,
...). A pipeline config points at a layout instead of hand-writing
stride/attribute tables — game code never spells out offsets.

  - Shader input locations are implicit: attribute i of the layout is
    location i, in declaration order starting at 0.
  - Multi-stream pipelines (keyframe morph) set vertex_streams = N;
    the layout is replicated per stream and locations continue
    sequentially (stream 1 of a 3-attribute layout is locations 3..5).
    Draw calls supply one mesh per stream, all sharing one topology.
  - Pipeline creation reflects the vertex shader's SPIR-V and HARD
    FAILS on any mismatch (missing attribute, wrong format), with the
    pipeline name and location in the log. A config mistake can no
    longer render silently wrong; an attribute the shader ignores is
    only a warning. Debug builds only (like the validation layers) —
    release builds don't even compile the reflector.
  - A draw call whose mesh count differs from the pipeline's declared
    stream count is rejected with an error at submission.


DECIDED: character authoring — rigid segments, not armatures
------------------------------------------------------------

The player model (resources/models/blender/player.blend) is built as a TREE
OF RIGID MESH OBJECTS, one per body part, each object's ORIGIN placed on the
joint it rotates around:

    m_pelvis  (root)
      m_torso
        m_head            m_hair, m_mustache
        m_arm_upper_*  ->  m_arm_lower_*  ->  m_hand_*
      m_leg_upper_*    ->  m_leg_lower_*  ->  m_foot_*

No armature, no skinning. Animation is object rotation, and the exporter
already bakes each object's evaluated world transform into its vertices per
keyframe, so a segmented rig costs nothing the format does not already pay.
Reasons this is the right call and not a shortcut:

  - it is the technique the target aesthetic came from. Alone in the Dark
    and its contemporaries animated rigid chunks; smooth skinned deformation
    on 900 triangles reads as neither 1992 nor modern.
  - joints stay watertight by OVERLAP, not by weighting: every child segment
    begins with a rounded ring that starts slightly inside its parent
    (t < 0 in the limb builder), so a bend rotates the overlap instead of
    opening a gap. This is the one thing to preserve when editing.
  - every segment being rigid is what makes rigid equipment anchors correct
    rather than approximate — a cuirass bolted to the torso anchor cannot
    drift, because the torso genuinely is one rigid body.
  - posing needs no rig UI: select the part, rotate. Nothing to maintain.

Joint sign conventions, because they are easy to get backwards and one of
them is not symmetric: a POSITIVE x rotation swings a limb BACKWARD. So a
forward step is negative on the thigh, a knee bend is POSITIVE on the shin
(knees fold backward), and an elbow bend is NEGATIVE on the forearm (elbows
fold forward). Positive z yaws toward the character's LEFT (+X), so a
right-handed coil is negative z on the torso and the strike swings it
positive. Getting the forearm sign wrong hyperextends every elbow in the
animation and is not obvious in a still frame.

Anchors (all seven exported, ~28 bytes per keyframe each, so the cost of
carrying unused ones is noise):

    hand.r  hand.l    weapons / shields
    head              helmets, hats
    torso             body armor
    back              cloaks, quivers, sheathed weapons
    foot.r  foot.l    boots

Grip convention, shared by every anchor and every weapon: the anchor's +Z is
the weapon's long axis out of the fist and its ±Y is the way the edges face,
so a weapon is authored UPRIGHT WITH ITS GRIP ON THE ORIGIN (the runtime's
matrix is player_world * interpolated_anchor, so whatever sits on the weapon's
origin lands in the hand). The hands rest at 148 degrees, i.e. blade hanging
down and about 30 degrees forward: straight down hides the weapon behind the
body at our camera pitch, straight out turns it into a lance.

Animations so far: idle (0.98 s, loops, first pose == last), walk_r and
walk_l (0.25 s each, ONE step per tile), attack (0.625 s, right-handed
diagonal slash), bump (0.375 s, one step into a wall, both hands brace, push
off and step back).

The walk is TWO animations, one leading with each leg, and the game
alternates them per tile. The first version packed a whole two-step stride
into one 0.25 s clip and read as jitter rather than walking: the body bobbed
twice, so at a 12 Hz export cadence each step got about three keyframes and
the bob ran at 8 Hz. One step per clip halves the frequency and doubles the
samples per motion for the same cost.

The cut is at the PASSING pose (legs nearly together, one knee lifted), not
at the contact pose. Cutting at contact would make each clip a textbook
half-cycle but would leave a stopped character frozen mid-stride; cutting at
passing leaves him nearly upright. walk_l is walk_r reflected across x=0 by
a mirror helper in the script rather than a second hand-authored table,
which is what guarantees the seam: each clip's end pose IS the other's start
pose, verified identical in world space.

One tile is about twice a natural step for this figure (a 1.26-unit-tall
model at PLAYER_SCALE 0.7 crossing a 1.0 tile), so the stride is authored
deliberately long and the feet still slide. Closing that gap entirely would
mean scaling the model up relative to the tile; not worth it.


DECIDED: the game sets an animation's duration, never the reverse
------------------------------------------------------------------

Gameplay timing constants are gameplay decisions. An animation must never
be the reason PLAYER_MOVE_SPEED has a particular value -- that does not
scale, because the moment monsters arrive every creature's attack length
would start dictating its own combat timing constant.

Built:

    ModelInstance_PlayAnimation(instance, animation)
    ModelInstance_PlayAnimationS(instance, animation, playback_speed)

The instance carries the speed and Update multiplies delta_time by it; plain
PlayAnimation is the 1.0 case. No format change was needed -- an animation's
authored length is already keyframes[keyframe_count - 1].time_s.

The multiplier is a ratio, so a call site pairing an animation to an action
computes authored_length / action_duration. For the walk that is
0.25 * PLAYER_MOVE_SPEED. Keep those two in view of each other: passing a
speed tuned against an older, differently-timed clip is a silent way to end
up back at jitter.

The payoff is bigger than tidiness. The client is already specified to
buffer events and play them at its own pace, fast-forwarding when the queue
is deep (see "message flow"), and a per-instance speed is exactly the
mechanism that makes fast-forward possible at all.

Caveat: scaling is uniform, so squashing a clip speeds up its wind-up and
its recovery equally. That is what every engine does and it is fine over a
modest range, but the authored length stays a "natural speed" hint -- push
far from it and the motion reads wrong. The API exists so a gameplay retune
does not force a re-export, not so one clip can cover 0.1 s and 1.0 s.


MILESTONE 2 (next): one room, walking cube
------------------------------------------

Protocol message structs + the local message pump + a server that
generates one room and resolves moves + the client walking a cube
around it. Proposed shape, hack on it:

    src/game/protocol/   protocol.h  command/event structs, ids
                         rules.c/h   shared pure predicates
    src/game/server/     server.h    Server_* API
    src/game/client/     client.h    Client_* API
    src/game/game_main.c local pump

meson: protocol_lib (core only), server_lib (core + protocol only —
this is the enforced moat), client + game_main stay in game_lib
(core, platform, engine, protocol).

Local transport = no abstraction at all. game_main pumps once per
frame:

    Client_Update()            input -> predicts -> queues command_t
    Client_PollCommands()      drain client outbox
    Server_HandleCommand()     resolve, world advances
    Server_PollEvents()        drain server outbox
    Client_HandleEvent()       fold into view, reconcile predictions

A future network build replaces the pump with socket code on each
side (and that is where ser/deser lives); client and server code are
untouched. The symmetry lives in the message structs, not in a
transport vtable we don't need yet.

v1 messages (sketch): command_t { type, sequence, union { move {dx,
dy} } }; event_t { type, union { snapshot { extents, terrain cells,
entities, player id }, entity_moved { id, from, to, caused_by_seq },
command_rejected { sequence, reason } } }. Fixed-cap inline arrays
per the struct rules. Terrain/entity ids in protocol.h.

First cut, decided 2026-09-27 (supersedes the sketch above where they
differ):

  - Directory is common/, not protocol/. Libraries: common_lib (core),
    server_lib (core + common), client_lib (core, platform, engine,
    common), game_lib = game_main.c only. The client getting its own
    lib means it cannot include server headers either.
  - Commands and events are separate types (revised the same day; a
    single shared event_t was tried first). command_t is client ->
    server: player intent, and every command advances the game one
    turn. event_t is server -> client: facts. The server's entry point
    is Server_HandleCommand, so it cannot be handed an event.
  - Commands carry a sequence number. COMMAND_MOVE is a direction
    {dx, dy}, not a target position. Intent is "step east", so the
    client cannot even express a teleport.
  - Turn boundary: the server finishes EVERY command with TURN_DONE
    {sequence}, whether the command was accepted or refused. Everything
    between the command and its TURN_DONE is that turn's result
    (later: monsters acting too). A refused command has no special
    event: it just produces no PLAYER_MOVED before its TURN_DONE. This
    also answers part of the open question on grouping events into
    world turns.
  - Shared tile_t { type, flags } and level_t in common/level.h. On the
    server FLAG_REVEALED means "already sent to the player" (its map
    memory, single player for now). On the client it means "known,
    draw it". TILE_REVEAL carries only the terrain type; server flags
    never cross the wire.
  - Startup: the server sends LEVEL_INIT (extents only), PLAYER_MOVED
    (initial placement), then TILE_REVEAL for every tile in the player's
    FOV (see below), and finally
    RUN_START. RUN_START closes the initial burst: the client's single
    run_active flag is set there, so run_active always implies the level
    and the player exist. Nothing is drawn and no input is taken before
    it. Separate "is the level ready" / "is the player placed" flags
    were tried and rejected. After each move it recomputes FOV and sends
    ONLY visible tiles not already revealed.
  - FOV (pulled forward from milestone 3), common/fov.c, one shared
    function Fov_Compute(level, x, y, radius). It clears and sets
    FLAG_VISIBLE on the level it is given. Circle of FOV_RADIUS 7
    (DCSS's default), in-radius test dx^2 + dy^2 <= r^2 + r for a rounder
    edge. A tile is visible if a Bresenham line from the origin to it,
    OR from it back to the origin, passes no sight-blocking tile. Only
    TILE_WALL blocks (Rules_BlocksSight). The blocking tile itself is
    visible. A second pass makes any in-radius wall that touches a
    visible open tile visible too. Without it, a wall the player stands
    next to is partly dark, because lines nearly parallel to it clip the
    adjacent wall tile.
  - Reveal margin (decided): the server reveals every visible tile PLUS
    every tile touching a visible OPEN (non-sight-blocking) tile, via
    Fov_TouchesVisibleOpenTile, shared with FOV's wall pass. That way
    every lit edge (view rim, shadow edges around corners, doorways) has
    fog next to it for the torn edge to tear into, instead of black.
    Neighbours of visible WALLS are deliberately excluded: those are the
    far side of the wall, and with our camera they would show as fog
    floor behind every wall, leaking the next room's layout. First tried,
    and rejected by the user: FOV at radius + 1. It still left black,
    edgeless tiles at every shadow edge. Accepted cost: one tile of
    terrain around corners and past doorways is revealed without being
    seen.
  - The server runs FOV on the true map and is the authority on what
    gets revealed. The client runs the SAME function on its own known
    map, purely to split revealed tiles into "visible now" and
    "remembered" (drawn in fog, see "Rendering: fog of war" below). The client recomputes lazily (fov_dirty), set ONLY
    where FOV inputs change: its known map (LEVEL_INIT, TILE_REVEAL) or
    its position (a predicted move, or any snap to a server position).
    Events that change neither must not set it. It computes from its
    predicted position, so the fog moves the instant you step. Unknown tiles are TILE_EMPTY on the
    client and don't block sight, which is harmless because they aren't
    drawn. This supersedes milestone 3's "memory streamed as remembered
    cell state" for terrain. Entity visibility (monsters) will still be
    the server's call, never inferred from the client's FOV.
  - PLAYER_MOVED is a fact, "the player is now at x,y", used for
    placement and movement alike.
  - Client prediction is an overlay, as the prediction section
    requires. server_player_x/y is the confirmed position and only
    ever comes from PLAYER_MOVED. player_pos_x/y is what is displayed
    and predicted. A move into a known non-walkable tile is a
    client-only bump: nothing is sent and no turn is spent. Any other
    move is predicted (walk starts instantly) and sent. On the TURN_DONE
    for the pending sequence the client compares its prediction with
    the confirmed position; a mismatch hard-snaps the display to it.
    A PLAYER_MOVED with nothing pending (the world moved you) snaps
    directly.
  - Later: TILE_REVEAL is one event per tile. Real FOV will want a
    batched reveal, since a room's worth of single events is chatty and
    would fill the 256-entry queue.
  - Rules_IsWalkable in common/rules.c is the shared predicate.
  - Memory lifetime: everything that lives as long as the current game
    ("the run": floors, the player, ...) goes in a run_arena, one on
    each side. Client and server each also keep a long-lived arena for
    things that outlive a run. For now a run is the whole process: each
    run_arena is created at init and never cleared, and LEVEL_INIT just
    allocates another level in it. Clearing it needs a "new run" signal,
    which doesn't exist yet.
  - Level gen (server/level_gen.c): NetHack-style. Three tile types
    only: TILE_EMPTY (nothing, not drawn), TILE_FLOOR, TILE_WALL.
    Rectangular rooms (up to 25x13). Separate rooms may share a wall,
    and a placement that overlaps exactly one room is sometimes kept,
    merging them into an irregular room. Rooms are joined by a minimum
    spanning tree over their center distances, so corridors stay short,
    plus some extra joins to the nearest unjoined room for loops. Each
    join is an L-shaped floor corridor. Corridors are ALWAYS 1 wide
    (decided), guaranteed by an odd grid: room interiors start and end
    on odd coordinates, and every corridor leg runs along an odd row or
    column. Two parallel corridors, or a corridor alongside a room, are
    therefore always at least 2 apart, with a wall between. Keep this
    invariant when editing the generator. Then every empty tile touching floor
    (8-neighbourhood) becomes wall. That walls rooms and corridors alike,
    and a doorway is simply the floor gap where a corridor crosses a
    room wall. No separate corridor or door tile types (decided: keep
    it simple). The player starts on a random odd tile in a random room. The seed
    comes from the clock and is logged. RNG is xorshift64* in
    core/rng.c.


DECIDED: rendering at a fixed low resolution
--------------------------------------------

The world renders into a static 640x360 target, never the window size,
for the 90s look. The swapchain pass integer-scales it to fit the
window (at least 1x) and centers it. 640x360 scales exactly to 720p
(x2), 1080p (x3), 1440p (x4) and 4K (x6). The camera aspect is the
target's, not the window's. Render targets therefore never resize with
the window, and the engine needs no resize path for them. Engine UI
(console, labels) is still drawn at window resolution in the swapchain
pass, after the scene.


DECIDED: rendering: fog of war
------------------------------

Three passes:
  1. fog mask (image pass, order 0): the tile geometry, into
     a UNORM 640x360 target (a linear format, because the values are
     data, not color). Each tile instance carries its tile coordinate and
     9 visibility bits: its own FLAG_VISIBLE and those of its 8
     neighbours. The shader writes 1 for visible tiles. For fogged tiles
     it computes the distance from the fragment to the nearest visible
     neighbour tile, and compares that against a world-anchored noise
     threshold (3D value noise on world position, so the pattern doesn't
     swim with the camera and wall faces tear too). The result is a torn
     edge that always lies INSIDE the first fogged tiles, never eating
     into the lit area. Visibility stays binary; the tear is purely
     rendering. Tiles next to VOID (neighbour unrevealed,
     TILE_EMPTY or off the map) get a second, independent torn edge on
     that side. It tears "known" to 0 (black) from the void side, using
     noise at an offset so the two edges don't mirror each other. So a
     fog tile between the lit area and the void has two torn edges. The
     void tear applies to EVERY tile, lit ones included (e.g. a visible
     wall with nothing known behind it gets a torn outer edge), so it
     doesn't depend on visibility at all. During a step, only the tear
     from neighbours that just got revealed shrinks away, with (1 - t). Instance bits:
     visibility = [0..8] visible now, [10..18] void now; previous =
     [0..8] visible before, 9 known before, [10..18] void before.
  2. scene (image pass, order 1, TWO targets): the sRGB 640x360 scene
     color (cleared), plus the fog mask LOADED from pass 1. Tile
     pipelines write only the color target. Entities write both, i.e.
     color plus a solid mask of lit = 1 and known = 1, in the same draw
     (color_output_count = 2).
  3. post (swapchain pass): a fullscreen quad blends the scene color
     towards the fog look by the mask. The fog look is desaturation
     then darkening, FOG_DESATURATION / FOG_BRIGHTNESS.

The edge is hard or soft by ONE knob, FOG_EDGE_SOFTNESS (in tiles). At
0 it is a hard torn line; above 0 it fades over that width past the
tear. Keep the hard option working: it's undecided which one we want.
FOG_TEAR_DEPTH sets how far into the first fog tile the tear can reach.
Invariant: the CENTER of every fogged tile is always fully fogged, so a
fogged tile can never read as "maybe in view". The nearest a visible
neighbour gets to a tile center is 0.5 tiles (an edge neighbour), so the
tear's total reach (depth + softness) must stay below that. The shader
enforces it with MAX_TEAR_REACH 0.45, clamping softness first and then
depth. Without the cap, a fogged tile with lit neighbours on two or more
sides was eaten from every side and looked almost lit.

Movement transitions (decided: directional sweep). Visibility changes the
moment a move is predicted, but the mask animates the change over the
walk, so the fog edge moves with the player instead of snapping:
  - The client snapshots all tile flags when it predicts a move
    (previous_flags, run arena), before any new reveals arrive. The
    progress t is the walk animation's progress. Standing still, bumps
    and snaps give t = 1, i.e. no transition.
  - Each mask instance carries the previous 9 visibility bits too, plus
    bit 9 = "was already revealed".
  - Tile coming into view: torn open from the previously visible side, with
    the tear threshold growing by t * 1.5 tiles.
    Tile leaving view: closed towards the still-visible side, the reverse.
    Fogged before and after: the tear from neighbours that stayed visible
    is untouched, the tear from lost neighbours shrinks with (1 - t), and
    the tear from new ones grows with t. So unchanged edges never flicker.
  - No neighbour to sweep from or towards (a corridor seen or lost
    through a doorway, far from the rest of the view): noise dissolve
    instead. That shows as brief speckles early in the step.
  - Newly revealed tiles that are NOT visible (the reveal-margin ring)
    dissolve from black to fog over the step. There's no directional
    info for them.
  - Mask green channel = "known". Tiles revealed for the first time open
    out of black with the same sweep instead of popping in. The post pass
    multiplies by it. Undrawn (unknown) tiles are 0, i.e. black.
Limit: the shader only sees each tile's 3x3 neighbourhood, so a sweep
covers one tile of travel. Anything further is the dissolve fallback.

Why tiles get a separate mask pass while entities write the mask from
the scene pass: tile pipelines stay unaware of fog, and the torn-edge
logic lives in one shader. The cost is that tile mask silhouettes only
match while the mask geometry matches the real geometry. Revisit if
props or unusual tile meshes ever show mismatched fog edges.

Entities (the player now, monsters later) must stamp a solid mask,
never trust the tile behind them. In a low close-up view the player
hides distant fogged tiles and would pick up their fog. They do it as a
second color output from the scene pass, not a second draw (decided: a
model is drawn exactly once). Because the stamp happens in the scene pass,
it uses the scene's depth buffer, so an entity hidden behind a wall
doesn't punch through the fog. Monsters are only drawn when visible, so
solid is always right for them.

Engine support this needed: a render pass takes up to
MAX_RENDERPASS_TARGETS (4) targets of equal size, each CLEAR or LOAD. A
LOAD target must have been rendered by an earlier pass that frame; it
is expected in SHADER_READ_ONLY. A pipeline writes the first
color_output_count targets of its pass (default 1); the rest are
write-masked. The mixed write masks need the independentBlend device
feature, which is now enabled.


MILESTONE 3 (agreed): monster, FOV, fog of war
----------------------------------------------

A simple monster that wanders the level, plus FOV and fog of war.
(Terrain FOV and fog of war were done early, in milestone 2. See
there.)
This is the milestone where the hidden-information machinery becomes
real: the server computes FOV and only sends visible cells; map
memory (fog of war) is tracked server-side per player and streamed as
"remembered" cell state. The wandering monster exercises the two
things milestone 2 can't: the world acting without any player command
(events not caused by the local client), and entities entering/
leaving the visible set (the client learns of the monster only when
it walks into view, and the server stops telling the client about it
the moment it leaves).


OPEN QUESTIONS
--------------

- Session model: server should think in terms of N attached clients
  per game (1 player + N spectators) even though locally N=1. When do
  we introduce the session/client-slot concept on the server side?

- Client-side smoothing: turn-based state jumps tile-to-tile; the 3d
  view wants short movement/attack animations. Client concern only,
  but affects how events are timestamped/grouped (e.g. "these events
  happened in one world turn" grouping for parallel animation).

- Replay format: with ser/deser living in the network layer, a replay
  is most naturally the recorded network byte stream. Fine — but it
  means replay recording is a network-layer feature, not available
  until the socket transport exists (or the local transport gains an
  optional record hook that runs the serializer).

- Persistence: save games are server-side state dumps. Format can wait
  until there is state worth saving.

- Directory name: settled on common/ (see milestone 2).
